{# Shared brief macros. Imported via `{% from "_macros.j2" import ... %}`.
   Available because LLMNode renders briefs through a Jinja Environment with
   FileSystemLoader pointing at this directory.

   Producers participating in a retry loop must add the gate-retry context
   to their render dict — see `gates._base.gate_retry_context`. The macro
   renders nothing on iteration 1 (no prior failure) so non-retry runs
   produce identical briefs to before. #}

{# HOM-377: render one verbatim canon block pulled live from the skill by the
   runtime canon loader (`_canon_loader.load_canon_blocks`). The `body` is the
   exact section text; never paraphrase it here — that is the whole point of
   pulling it at run time instead of embedding a copy that rots. `title` is a
   human label (skill + §section) for the sub-agent and the snapshot reviewer. #}
{%- macro canon_section(title, body) -%}
=== CANON — VERBATIM from the live skill: {{ title }} (follow exactly; do not summarize) ===
{{ body }}=== end CANON: {{ title }} ===
{% endmacro -%}

{%- macro prior_violations_block(prior_violations, prior_iteration) -%}
{%- if prior_violations and prior_iteration -%}

## Previous attempt (iteration {{ prior_iteration }}) failed these checks; address them on this attempt:

{% for v in prior_violations -%}
- {{ v }}
{% endfor %}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}

{# HOM-294: shared palette + typography hard-rule blocks used by p4_beat.j2
   and p4_redispatch_beat.j2. Hoisted verbatim from p4_beat.j2 so the
   redispatch brief — invoked precisely when gate:design_adherence fired —
   surfaces the same rules the sub-agent just violated. #}
{%- macro design_adherence_hard_rules() -%}
- **Palette discipline (strict ⊆).** Use ONLY hexes from `state.compose.design.palette[*].hex` (already pre-baked with gradient-stop / highlight derivatives by `p4_design_system`). No improvised gradient stops, no shade variants, no near-palette derivatives. The HF canon `gate:design_adherence` (`~/.agents/skills/hyperframes/SKILL.md` §"Design Adherence" item 1) rejects any hex not literally in the palette set. If a needed shade isn't in the palette, choose the nearest palette entry — do NOT invent a 5-6 unit-off-channel variant. If you genuinely need an in-between value, prefer alpha-blending via `rgba(...)` (a literal palette hex parsed to channels + an alpha) or `tl.set/.to(..., {opacity: ...})`; both are gate-clean because the gate hex-extracts only `#RRGGBB` / `#RRGGBBAA` tokens.
  ```css
  /* ❌ ALL three stops missing from palette — gate:design_adherence will fail */
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, #2a221c 0%, #1f1a16 55%, #14100e 100%);
  /* ✅ every stop is a literal palette[*].hex — pre-baked by p4_design_system */
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--background) 0%, var(--background-stop-1) 55%, var(--background-stop-2) 100%);
  ```
- **Typography fallback discipline.** In any `font-family` stack, use ONLY:
  - named families from `state.compose.design.typography[*].family` (Playfair Display, Inter — whatever DESIGN.md declares),
  - generic CSS keywords (`serif`, `sans-serif`, `monospace`),
  - the system-UI whitelist (`system-ui`, `-apple-system`).

  Do NOT add named fallbacks like Georgia, Helvetica, Times New Roman. `gate:design_adherence` (`~/.agents/skills/hyperframes/SKILL.md` §"Design Adherence" item 2) flags any token not in `typography[*].family` ∪ generics ∪ whitelist. Web fonts loaded by the root scaffold render reliably across the capture engine; named fallbacks would only fire if web-loading fails (it doesn't), and would render differently on different operator machines (Win/Mac may have Georgia, Linux Chromium resolves to Liberation/DejaVu Serif — divergent video output).
  ```css
  /* ❌ "Georgia" not in typography list — gate:design_adherence will fail */
  font-family: "Playfair Display", "Georgia", serif;
  /* ✅ design family + generic keyword */
  font-family: "Playfair Display", serif;
  ```
{%- endmacro -%}
